---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DividendCalculator from '@/components/tools/calc/DividendCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="dividend-calculator">
  <DividendCalculator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the dividend calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      The dividend calculator follows your shares year by year. The share price starts at 1, so the first
      money buys one share per unit. Each year your addition buys shares at that year's price,
      <code>(1 + p)^(n − 1)</code>, where <code>p</code> is the price growth. At the end of the year each
      share pays <code>d(n) = y × (1 + g)^(n − 1)</code>, where <code>y</code> is the dividend yield and
      <code>g</code> is the dividend growth.
    </p>
    <p>
      The income after tax is <code>D(n) = shares × d(n) × (1 − tax)</code>. With reinvesting on, each
      dividend after tax buys more shares at the price at the end of the year. The value of the holding is
      the shares times the price.
    </p>
    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      10,000 to start and 1,200 added each year, at a 4% yield, 5% dividend growth, 4% price growth and 15%
      tax. In year 1 the income is (10,000 + 1,200) × 4% × 0.85 = 380.80. With dividends reinvested, the
      income in year 20 is 3,860.97, or 321.75 a month. You receive 33,491.74 of dividends over the 20
      years, and the holding is worth 102,327 against 34,000 put in. Without reinvesting, the income in year
      20 is 2,316.37.
    </p>
    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <p>
      The yield, dividend growth and price growth stay the same every year; real companies can cut their
      dividend. Dividends are paid once a year, at the end of the year. Tax on selling, fees and inflation
      are not included.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
